{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"agent_message","text":"I must be careful here: the end-to-end probe revealed the entry was rewritten to EntryNormal, so it does not support a commit claim. Recording no new evidence this turn.\n\n{\"submission\":\"update.json\",\"summary\":\"No new submission this turn, deliberately. I tried to close the remaining limitation on the conf-change finding - that the apply step was driven directly rather than after a genuine commit - by driving a real three-node network. The attempt did not succeed and produced counterevidence I must not misread as support: in that flow the leader's applied index had not advanced past the entering joint change, so the propose-time alreadyPending guard fired and rewrote the entry to EntryNormal (entry_type reported EntryNormal at the committed index), meaning the malformed change was dropped rather than committed, and the panic I observed came from invoking applyConfChange directly on the change object, exactly the shortcut the limitation already flags. Closing the gap properly requires driving the application side (Ready and Advance) so the applied index catches up before proposing the malformed change; I did not complete that within this turn. A second, narrower harness fact also emerged: the test network helper cannot represent a configuration containing an id that is absent from the network, which is why the first attempt panicked inside the helper rather than in the library. Nothing in the recorded findings changes: the draft update file is resubmitted unchanged, so the two asserted defects and their receipts stand as previously recorded.\"}"}}
